<Base title="About" description="What Iron Archive is, why it exists, and how to contribute.">
  <h1>About Iron Archive</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    A public reference for strength-training machine specifications — especially the
    discontinued models where no manufacturer documentation survives.
  </p>

  <h2>The facts nobody publishes</h2>
  <p>
    Two numbers decide how a machine actually trains you, and neither is in the
    manual:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Starting weight</strong> — the real load with the pin at the lowest
      plate, carriage and linkage included.</li>
    <li><strong>Leverage ratio</strong> — what the muscle actually feels, not the
      number stamped on the stack. On plate-loaded and cam machines the difference
      is large enough to make cross-gym comparison meaningless without it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There's no single "key number" for every machine — the right one depends on how
    it loads (a leverage <em>curve</em> for cams, pulley ratio for cables,
    counterbalance for a Smith, and so on). Iron Archive collects these,
    field-measured and openly sourced.
  </p>

  <h2>How it's built to stay honest</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Provenance on every value</strong> — who measured it, when, how, and
      how sure.</li>
    <li><strong>Disagreements are shown, not averaged.</strong> Two different
      readings both display, flagged for a tiebreak. Nothing is silently
      overwritten or collapsed to one number.</li>
    <li><strong>Unknowns are visible</strong> — a missing value is an open invitation
      to add it, not a hidden gap.</li>
    <li><strong>Generations are separate.</strong> A model's 1978 original and its
      2010s remake are different machines, kept apart so their measurements don't
      contaminate each other.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Contributing</h2>
  <p>
    Contributors are gym people with phones, not developers. When the submission
    form is live, the “measure one / add it” links across the site open a prefilled
    submission that becomes a reviewed pull request — you never touch Git. A photo
    and a guessed model name is already a legitimate record.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you're taking measurements, please follow the
    <a href="https://github.com/anthonygwalters/iron-archive/blob/main/docs/protocol.md">measurement protocol</a>:
    a hanging scale, a stated range-of-motion position, three trials recorded
    individually.
  </p>

  <h2>Open by design</h2>
  <p>
    The whole project is public: the records, the schemas, and the code live in the
    <a href="https://github.com/anthonygwalters/iron-archive">GitHub repository</a>.
    There's no database and no admin panel — the pull-request list is the review
    queue.
  </p>
</Base>

